# Design: the floor. What the bootstrap is and the choices made writing it. spec/core-operations.md is the authority for the operations and spec/framebuffer.md for its memory.

n: 5.14
name: floor
kind: hub
is: The bootstrap: bootstrap/ck.c and the layout bootstrap/fb.h, under the 2,000-line budget. All of its memory is one shared region, the framebuffer, that a second process records and replays. On it stand a store, the canonical reader and printer, a walker over 54 operations whose registers are a record in the region and whose operations and step a running program can replace, the birth of code pages and a table of ten host functions. Zero and one are catalogue entries and nothing is the absence of a node.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.1
name: operations-are-catalogue-entries
kind: decision
is: An operation is a catalogue entry from 32 up, and a composite whose blueprint is that entry is executed; every other node answers itself. Entries 32 to 99 are the bootstrap's: control with backtracking, arithmetic, native code, the floor's own record kinds, a node's parts and bytes, binding and layers, reading and printing, the step, an outcome, a fault, cells and notes, and the region itself. The page names each. From 128 up entries are named by modules written in form (5.15.1); 128 is compile (5.14.26).
status: inferred
from: 5.13.3 5.4.6 5.1.2
src: 2.30.6
conf: mid
limit: Operations that hold an offer or bind a cell are not named, and none works on the bits of a number. The count of about 25 in 5.1.5 is passed at 54.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.2
name: zero-and-one-are-entries
kind: decision
is: The acknowledgment 0 is catalogue entry 32 and the acknowledgment 1 is entry 33, both nodes and both ordinary answers. Nothing is no node at all: the handle 0 in memory and the id of 32 zero bytes on the wire. The integer zero is a different node from the answer of no.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.13 5.13.2 1.1
src: 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: A number that doubles as a verdict is how one marker came to carry several readings in the old body.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.14.3
name: need-turns-a-no-into-a-fail
kind: decision
is: A comparison answers yes or no and never fails. The operation need fails when its child answers no or nothing. A condition is therefore written need of a comparison, and 'if c then a else b' is a choice whose first option is a sequence of cut(need c) and a.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.3 5.4.13
src: 2.30.6
conf: mid
alt: Comparisons that fail when false, as in Icon; that would leave the answer of no with almost no use.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.14.4
name: cut-scope
kind: decision
is: Cut closes the nearest enclosing choice in the same recipe body and every choice opened since it was entered; with none, the choices opened since the running recipe was called. All is a barrier a cut does not cross.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.6 5.4.7 3.5.2.5
src: 2.30.6
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.14.5
name: self-for-direct-recursion
kind: decision
is: A recipe reaches itself through the operation self, which answers the recipe now running. A node cannot hold its own id, so this is how the floor recurses. Recipes that name each other remain question 6.1.14.
status: inferred
from: 5.2.2 5.13.1
src: 2.30.6
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.14.6
name: walker-frames-never-change
kind: decision
is: The walker uses no C recursion and keeps its registers in the region's machine record. A continuation is a chain of frames that are never altered once made, and a choice point keeps a frame index and four allocation marks. Fail resets the marks and resumes from the kept frame, so a choice that has answered can be re-entered. A frame is released early only when no choice point can return to it.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.9 5.7.2 5.4.7 5.7.6
src: 2.30.7
conf: mid
limit: A program has no cell it can change in place, so there is no trail yet. A choice point's next option is the one field the walker changes in place.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.7
name: floor-interns-by-content
kind: decision
is: In the floor every node is interned when made, by a cheap hash over its kind, blueprint handle and child handles or bytes, with equality decided by content. The content id is computed only when asked for: at freeze, thaw, a print of an id, or a check. 5.2.3 proposes leaving transient nodes without interning; the floor interns every node.
status: inferred
from: 5.7.3 5.2.3 3.15.8.5
src: 2.30.7
conf: mid
why: One representation keeps the floor small, and equal nodes sharing a handle makes eq a comparison of two integers.
flip: Measured allocation cost in the walker once a compiler runs on it.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.14.8
name: ice-file
kind: decision
is: An ice file is the eight bytes 'CKICE1', a line feed and a zero, and then records, each a 32-bit little-endian length and one node's canonical bytes. Freeze writes each node once: its blueprint, then its children in order, then the node, so the top node is last. The root is left out unless it is the node frozen. Thaw reads records in order, refuses a record whose blueprint or child it has not yet seen, and computes each id from the bytes it read. A write goes to a temporary file that is renamed.
status: inferred
from: 5.7.1 5.13.1
src: 2.30.7
conf: mid
limit: The file is read whole into the region's text area and its nodes are made in the store; it is not read where it lies as 5.7.1 proposes. There is no index.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.9
name: host-functions-so-far
kind: decision
is: Ten host functions exist, each one entry in a table: reserve the shared region, reserve the code region, switch this thread between writing and running code, flush the instruction cache, read a file into the region, call a function, read a clock, map another process's region to be read, look up a symbol, and hand a fault of the processor to a handler on its own stack. A program reaches the file, the clock and, by name, any function of the host as operations (5.14.40). Thread start in 5.8.1 is not written.
status: inferred
from: 5.8.1 5.9.4
src: 2.30.7
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.10
name: floor-is-a-framebuffer
kind: observation
is: All of the bootstrap's memory is one POSIX shared region laid out by bootstrap/fb.h: the node table, payloads, both indexes, frames, links, activations, arguments, choice points, gathered answers, the reader's and printer's working lists, every file it reads, and the machine stack. It keeps no heap. The walker's registers are the region's machine record. Observed on 2026-11-25: the 142 encoding vectors hold and the 27 ground programs answer as expected.
status: observed
src: 2.30.7 2.30.9 2.30.11
see: 5.14.12 5.14.13
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.11
name: record-kinds-are-entries
kind: decision
is: Every record in the region starts with the handle of its blueprint and the handle of the program node that last wrote it. The kinds of the floor's own records are catalogue entries 53 to 63 and 72: framebuffer, area, machine, node, frame, link, activation, choice-point, gathered, words, bytes and binding. A reader of the region therefore needs no table that is not in the catalogue.
status: inferred
from: 5.7.7 5.7.4 5.13.3
src: 2.30.9 2.30.6
conf: mid
limit: A record in a table of words or bytes is a bare value described by its area's entry, not by a tag of its own. The catalogue's names are still held outside any node, in the specification.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.12
name: what-lies-outside-the-region
kind: observation
is: Tested on the first machine on 2026-11-25: macOS refuses a shared executable mapping with EINVAL, so the runnable copy of a code leaf is in a private mapping; the region records the leaf, the offset of its copy and that mapping's address. Also outside: machine registers, what the dynamic loader and the thread library keep, the process's arguments, and the first thread's stack until the bootstrap moves onto the region's stack.
status: observed
src: 2.30.11 2.30.7
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.13
name: framebuffer-cost
kind: observation
is: Measured on the first machine on 2026-11-25 with ground/long-recursion.ck, 158.6 million walker steps. The run took 0.71 to 0.72 s alone in five runs; recorded by a second process every 5 ms, 0.71 to 0.76 s in three; every 1 ms, 0.72 to 0.75 s in five, giving 481 frames.
status: observed
src: 2.30.11 2.30.7 2.30.10
limit: One program on one machine with other work running. A build of the same walker with its registers in C locals and its tables on a private heap ran this program in 0.56 s, so keeping everything in the region costs the walker about a quarter of its speed here.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.14
name: returned-calls-give-back
kind: decision
is: A call in the last position of a recipe keeps no frame. When the walker returns through a frame, the activations and arguments made since that frame was pushed are given back, unless an open choice was made after them. The links that gathered an operation's values are given back when the operation runs, under the same rule. A long recursion therefore holds memory in proportion to its depth, not to its number of calls.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.6 5.7.2
src: 2.30.7
conf: mid
why: A recorded frame copies every used byte, so memory that grows with the number of calls would make every frame grow with it.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.15
name: a-nodes-parts
kind: decision
is: Seven operations, entries 64 to 70, reach everything the encoding says a node is. blueprint, count, child, byte and leaf take a node apart: its blueprint, how many bytes or children, child k, byte k as a number, and whether it is a leaf. pack and join build: the leaf of a blueprint and a list of numbers, the composite of a blueprint and a list's children. Positions and bytes are int leaves, the kind arithmetic already takes.
status: inferred
from: 5.13.1 5.14.1 7.13
src: 2.30.6 2.30.7
conf: mid
why: Code is to be written as nodes (7.13), and a program that reads, checks or compiles nodes must first reach their parts. Each of the seven is one fact of section 1 or 2 of the encoding; none can be made from the others without a fault standing in for an answer.
flip: A home lens may want one operation that matches a node against a pattern in place of the five that take it apart.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.16
name: lists-carry-the-parts
kind: decision
is: pack and join take their parts as one list, and a program makes that list with all over a recipe that answers one part and, failed back into, the next. So a node of any size is built with two arguments, and the walker's bound of eight children for an operation does not bound what can be built.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.15 5.14.6
src: 2.30.6
conf: mid
flip: Building through all costs one choice point and one gathered record a part; a compiler may need a builder that appends.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.17
name: a-refused-build-answers-nothing
kind: decision
is: pack and join go through the store's one way of making a node, so a built node is the one node with that content and carries the pack or join node as its writer. A build the encoding refuses answers nothing and the reason is the refusal's word: packing the bytes 01 00 under nat answers nothing with 'minimal'. A program cannot make a node the reader would refuse.
status: inferred
from: 5.13.6 5.14.7 5.7.7
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: high
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.18
name: first-programs-on-a-nodes-parts
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-25 with 'make check': ground/rebuild.ck takes a nested node apart to its bytes and builds it again, and answers the node it was given. ground/nat-rule.ck, the shortest-form rule of a nat kept as nodes, answers for five candidates the leaves 0, 42 and 256 and twice the word 'minimal'. Read from its region by a second process, the leaf 256 is a record written by the node under entry 69, pack, and the answer by the all node.
status: observed
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6 2.30.10
limit: The session that wrote nat-rule.ck had read the bootstrap's own rule, so it is not a second hand's work. What the region holds was read by hand.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.19
name: an-operation-is-a-binding
kind: decision
is: The bootstrap's C is one implementation of an operation, the one a cold start runs. bind gives any operation, any unnamed entry or any recipe another, and the implementation says how it is used: a recipe takes the children's answers, a lazy takes the node unevaluated and so can stand for a control operation, a code leaf takes integers and may decline to what lies beneath, and a leaf of another blueprint is run by what that blueprint is bound to. up and base evaluate a node where the implementation's user stands, in that user's cut scope. A binding is a record in the region.
status: inferred
from: 7.13 5.9.6 5.9.7 5.7.7 5.14.1
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Any part is to be replaceable by a form recipe, by machine code and by what is invented later (7.13). One table the walker asks before it runs its own code serves all three, and the observer reads it.
flip: Compiled code will want the lookup resolved once, not on every evaluation; the walker finds it through an index by node, each binding naming the one before it for the same entry.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.20
name: an-implementation-runs-on-what-is-beneath-it
kind: decision
is: Bindings are kept in the order they were made, and an activation carries how many of them it runs on. An implementation, and whatever it calls, sees only the bindings older than its own. So a new add uses the add it replaces without calling itself, add and sub are each written with the other and both end in the bootstrap, and replacing an operation never changes the meaning of the code that implements it.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.19 5.14.6
src: 2.30.7
conf: mid
flip: An implementation that should see later bindings of other entries, as an optimizer replacing call might, needs a rule finer than one number.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.21
name: a-binding-is-undone-by-fail
kind: decision
is: A choice point keeps the number of bindings beside its other marks, and a fail that returns to it takes away every binding made since. A program can therefore try an implementation inside an option and lose it when the option fails, as the guide's definition of fail asks for every change.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.2 5.14.6 5.14.19
src: 2.30.7
conf: high
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.22
name: operations-in-form-and-in-machine-code
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-25. add, sub, mul and lt are written in form and as AArch64 leaves under form/. Run by hand with 'ck run F B', any one of the eight in place of the bootstrap's own left 25 ground programs answering the same. The long recursion answers 2178309 on the four form versions at once in 9.3 s, on the four machine-code leaves in 0.80 s, and on the bootstrap's own in 0.75 s in the same minute. ground/on-form.ck and ground/on-code.ck bind all four from inside a program and run a recursion on them.
status: observed
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6 2.30.10
limit: These leaves are written by hand. A wrong form recipe under a recursion gives no answer and runs until an area is full.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.23
name: what-cannot-be-replaced-yet
kind: limit
is: Every operation, every recipe and the step can be replaced; only add, sub, mul and lt are written in form, the step in machine code takes only leaves, and the emitter compiles only numbers (5.14.26). The store, freeze, thaw and the other host functions are not operations and have no binding. A replaced step decides what a node means, and the walker still carries the run between nodes: the continuation, the choice points and the undo are its own. A binding is made only by the running program, lasts until a fail takes it away, and has no scope of its own.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.19 5.14.24 7.13 5.9.3
conf: high
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.24
name: the-step-is-an-entry
kind: decision
is: Evaluating one node is the walker's step, and the step is entry 81. Used, it runs a node a program holds. Bound, its implementation is given every node about to be evaluated, leaves and data included: a lazy or a recipe gets the node, and code gets the node's handle and its activation. The newest binding that applies to a node runs it, its blueprint's or the step's; base, or code that declines, hands the node to what lies beneath. A tracing, a compiling or a remote evaluator is then one more implementation.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.19 5.14.20 7.13 7.21 5.9.6
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: One table already says what runs for each operation. Putting the step in it means nothing about evaluation is outside what a running program can exchange, and an emitter can start with the node kinds it knows and decline the rest (5.10.4).
flip: A compiled evaluator that runs whole recipes without returning to the walker will want to be asked once per recipe, not once per node.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.25
name: the-step-in-form-and-in-machine-code
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-25. A marking step in form left 17 marks for the 17 distinct nodes the small recursion evaluates, and the answer was the same. A step that reads every 2 as 3 changed the recursion's answer from 55 to 32, and a fail took it away. A step of ten hand-written AArch64 instructions read each node's record in the region, answered the leaves and declined the rest; over a form step that turns every leaf into a word, the program still answered 55. The long recursion took 0.75 s on the walker alone, 1.29 s under the code step and 8.1 s under the marking step, two runs each.
status: observed
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6 2.30.10
limit: The marks were counted by hand from the region's node count with and without the step. The code step compiles nothing: it is a socket shown to hold.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.26
name: an-emitter-in-form
kind: decision
is: Entry 128, compile, has no code in the bootstrap; form/compile.aarch64.ck binds it to an emitter written in form, a layer of named definitions. One pass over a recipe's body gathers each node's instructions as bytes and packs them into one code leaf, bound to the recipe or to the entry asked for. It knows numbers: any int leaf, arguments, let, the five operations, tests of lt and eq joined by if, and a call of the recipe itself by self or by name (5.14.48). For any other it emits a byte that pack refuses, so a recipe is compiled whole or stays on the walker.
status: inferred
from: 5.10.4 5.14.19 5.14.15 7.13 7.21
src: 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: The emitter is to be a program in the language (5.10.4), and the walker already gives it what it needs: a node's parts, arithmetic, all and pack. Dividing by 256 is done by reading the bytes of a product, since no operation works on bits.
flip: Per-node hand-back to the walker, as 5.10.4 describes, needs emitted code to call the walker; until then a recipe with one unknown node is not compiled at all.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.27
name: a-recipe-runs-on-code-the-emitter-wrote
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-25. For the recursion of ground/recursion.ck the emitter wrote a leaf of 67 instructions. On it the long recursion answered 2178309 in 0.02 s and 7,945 walker steps, loading and compiling included, where the walker alone takes 0.75 s and 158.6 million. A million calls of a*a + b*3 - 7 took 0.24 s compiled and 0.50 s on the walker. In seventeen trials over six recipes the answer, the reason of a nothing and the fault were the walker's: overflows, nested and inside a condition, a missing, a text and a nothing argument, and recursions a million deep.
status: observed
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6 2.30.10
limit: Six recipes, one machine, trials by hand and not kept as programs.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.28
name: emitted-code-answers-a-number-or-declines
kind: decision
is: Every call of code carries the region's address in its seventh register and the count of its values in its eighth, and code is not asked when a value is not an integer. Emitted code declines when an overflow is left at its end, when it was given fewer arguments than the recipe reads, and when the stack, whose end it reads from the region, has no room. The decline travels up through its calls and the walker runs the whole call on the bindings beneath that code. Observed: a recursion a million deep answers in 0.15 s, 0.13 s on the walker alone.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.26 5.14.19 5.14.20 5.7.6
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Code that is faster must not be different. Where it cannot be sure, the answer, the reason and the fault are the walker's own, at the cost of doing the work twice. Declining a whole call is the hand-back that needs nothing new: the walker already runs what a binding declines.
flip: Code that goes on computing while it unwinds wastes work in a branching recursion; a jump straight to the leaf's end would not.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.29
name: a-program-observes-an-outcome
kind: decision
is: Entry 82, outcome, evaluates a node to its first outcome and answers a list of a word and a node: node and the answer, nothing and the reason, or fault and the reason. What the node did is undone as by a fail, bindings included. With it the check of the ground programs is a program: ground/check.ck reads each program's path and expected outcome from the node in ground/expect.ck, runs it, and answers those that ended otherwise. Observed on 2026-11-25: it answers the empty list over 32 programs in 0.8 s, and a wrong expectation planted for 42 named the three programs that answer 42.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.2 5.14.21 5.14.24 7.13 5.11.4
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: The reason beside a nothing could be printed by the bootstrap and read by no program, so only a shell loop over printed lines could judge a run. An outcome held as a node is compared by identity, and the table of expected lines becomes a node too.
see: 5.14.31
limit: The expectations are written in the canonical print, which nobody reads easily; ground/observe.ck answers the list as the programs stand now, to be read against the old one before it is stored. The check's own answer is still compared by one line of the Makefile.
at: 2026-11-25


n: 5.14.30
name: bindings-made-together-are-one-layer
kind: decision
is: A binding's implementation runs on the bindings beneath its own (5.14.20), which keeps a replacement from meeting itself and so forbids definitions that call each other or themselves by name. Entry 86, layer, evaluates a node and then lets every binding made inside it run on all of them, with what was bound before beneath. Each binding record says how many bindings its implementation sees. A set of definitions is written as one layer; one bind alone keeps the older meaning.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.20 5.14.19 6.1.14 7.13
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Found by running: the first lens, bound entry by entry, could not call its own helpers. Entries bound in one layer name each other through the table, so a cycle of definitions needs no cycle of nodes, which is one answer to how mutual recursion lives in a hashed store (6.1.14).
flip: A layer that should stay open, so later definitions join it, needs more than a mark set when it closes.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.31
name: a-program-raises-its-own-fault
kind: decision
is: Entry 85, fault, breaks off with a reason the program gives, any node, exactly as the walker's own faults do: through every all and choice to the nearest recover or outcome. A fail turns execution around and is absorbed by an all; a fault is for what must not be absorbed, such as text a lens cannot read (5.3.8).
status: inferred
from: 5.3.8 5.4.6 5.14.29
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Without it a reader written in form could only fail, and a fail inside a gathering loses the reason and reads as the end of the list.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.32
name: the-encoding-and-ice-are-programs
kind: decision
is: Entries 87 and 88, encode and decode, are a node's canonical bytes and the node such bytes are. With read, print and id they are all the encoding's page fixes, so its conformance check (ground/vectors.ck over the vectors as a node), freeze and thaw (ice/ice.ck) are programs in form, and the bootstrap keeps two commands, run and text. Observed on 2026-11-25: the form check holds all 142 vectors and names planted wrong ones; the form freeze wrote the same 1,875 bytes as the C freeze it replaced, and its file thawed in a second process to the same node.
status: inferred
from: 5.13 5.14.7 7.21 7.13 5.11.4
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6 2.30.3
conf: mid
why: What is an operation can be replaced and used by a program; what was a command could be neither. Each command taken out is C that no longer has to be right.
limit: The two-process check is still one line of the Makefile's shell.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.33
name: code-reads-two-fixed-places-of-the-head
kind: decision
is: Code that reads the region may count on two places in its head whatever else moves: at byte 28 the floor, the lowest page the machine stack may reach, and at byte 152 where the nodes area starts. Emitted code reads the floor for its stack check. Found by review: it had read the stack's entry in the area table by position, and adding an area before it made that entry another area's with no check failing. ground/deep-on-code.ck now runs a recursion 600,000 deep, more than the stack holds, on emitted code.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.28 5.7.6 5.14.10
src: 2.30.7 2.30.9 2.30.11
conf: mid
flip: A leaf that needs more of the region than these two places wants a table it is handed, as 5.10.9 describes, not more fixed bytes.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.34
name: a-cell-holds-a-node-and-has-a-trail
kind: decision
is: Entries 89 and 90, hold and held, give a program memory it can change: any node is a cell, and it holds a node. While a choice is open, what a cell held before a change goes on the trail, and a fail, the end of an all or of an outcome puts it back; with no choice open nothing is trailed. Observed on 2026-11-25: with its words found through cells the lens's round trip of 59 files fell from 0.37 s to 0.20 s, and freezing the lens from 0.18 s to 0.04 s with the same file. An index built lazily inside an option was undone and rebuilt at every use, 11.7 s, until it was built when the lens loads.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.8 5.4.9 5.4.2 5.1.3 7.6 5.14.21
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Fail undoes every change (5.4.2), so memory that changes needs its trail from the first day. A program written in form had no table it could update, so every lookup was a search.
flip: 5.4.9 trails only cells older than the choice point; this trail records every change while a choice is open.
limit: A cell is only a holder. The declared blueprint, access and constructor recipes of 5.1.3, views, and state exempt from undo are not built.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.35
name: a-run-watches-itself-and-changes-what-runs-it
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-25. form/heat.ck, written in the lens, puts a lazy on call that counts each call in a note under its recipe. At the hundredth it steps off call, has the emitter compile that recipe, and takes its place again above the code; after ten thousand calls that bring nothing new it rests. Under it three recursions ran in one program: two ended on machine code, the third, which the emitter does not know, stayed on the walker. The answers were the walker's, in 0.03 s and 1.6 million walker steps; the first alone takes 158.6 million on the walker.
status: observed
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6 2.30.10
limit: Three recipes, one machine. The watcher sees only call, and pays its own cost on every call it sees.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.36
name: notes-are-outside-undo-and-a-binding-can-be-released
kind: decision
is: Entry 92, note, gives a cell a node that no fail undoes: what is observed about a run is true whether or not the path that produced it was kept. Entry 93, unbind, takes an entry's newest binding out of its chain with a record that points back at it, so a fail restores it. A watcher needs both: counts that outlast backtracking, and a way to leave once it has done its work.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.8 5.14.34 5.14.19 7.13 7.21
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: 5.4.8 keeps apart what is journaled and what has crossed the boundary of what is undone. The guidance asks that a diagnostic can be put in for a while and taken out again while the system runs.
flip: A binding removed from the middle of an entry's chain, or by name, needs more than the newest.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.37
name: the-region-is-described-by-a-node-and-read-through-it
kind: decision
is: What each record of the region looks like is a node, region/layout.ck: fields in order, their widths, and whether each holds a handle, a number or bytes. Entries 94 to 96, peek, handle and at, let a program read its own region, and region/region.ck reads it through that description: a field of a record, a view of the region as text with every binding in words, and a check of the description against the running region. Observed on 2026-11-25: the check finds nothing, and names a field that was widened and two that were changed over.
status: inferred
from: 5.7.6 5.7.7 5.14.10 5.14.33 6.1.11 7.13
src: 2.30.7 2.30.9 2.30.11
conf: mid
why: The region was to carry its blueprints, and the layouts lived only in a C header: emitted code read a record by a position typed in by hand and a change of layout broke it with no check failing (5.14.33). A layout that is a node can be read, checked and used to compute where a field lies.
flip: A leaf of machine code that needs a field's place should take it from the description, as a program in form now can; the emitter still carries two numbers.
limit: Field order and width inside a record are checked only by the header being made from the same node.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.38
name: the-c-header-is-made-from-nodes
kind: decision
is: bootstrap/fb.h is written by a program in form, region/header.ck, from two nodes: the lexicon, which gives each catalogue entry its number and word, and the layout of the region's records. The stored header must equal what they generate, and make check holds it to that. The C sources use the names that come out, so an operation, an area or a field is added in a node and the C follows. Before, the numbers were kept by hand in three places and the layouts in two.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.37 5.15.1 7.13 5.9.1
src: 2.30.9 2.30.7 2.30.14
conf: mid
why: New code is to be generated from its description, and a number written twice by hand drifts: the observer's table of words had fallen behind the catalogue more than once in a day.
limit: The header is the only C that is generated. A cold start still needs the stored copy, since making it needs the bootstrap it belongs to.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.39
name: an-observer-written-in-form
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-25. Entry 97, look, reads bytes of a region another process made, mapped to be read only. region/watch.ck, the same showing program that reads its own region, was pointed at the region a finished run had kept: it showed the version, the machine's step, each area in use and all thirteen bindings, the last four as watched, released, compiled and released, in 0.04 s. Each handle met was built again as a node here from the other process's records, so the bindings showed in the lens's words where the observer in C shows numbers for what it has no name for.
status: observed
src: 2.30.7 2.30.9 2.30.10 2.30.11
limit: One frame of a region kept after its run. Recording a run in motion and replaying it are still the observer in C.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.40
name: the-host-is-called-by-name
kind: decision
is: Entry 98, host, looks a name up in the host and calls what it finds with up to six arguments, an int as it is and any other leaf as the address of its bytes: one lookup and one raw call for the C library and a framework alike (5.8.3). What the host does cannot be undone, so the call is a fault, irreversible, while any choice is open. Observed on 2026-11-25: with no choice open a program in form wrote a file through creat, write and close, and found the host's getpid equal to the pid in its own region's head; inside an outcome the call was refused.
status: inferred
from: 5.8.1 5.8.3 5.4.8 5.9.3 7.3
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: 5.4.8 refuses an irreversible effect inside an open choice unless it is buffered until the commit. A direct call is refused; a call held back with after (5.14.50) is the buffering.
flip: A host function's answer is not available to a search, since a call held back answers only that it was held.
limit: Arguments go in registers only, so a function taking a varying number of them does not get those; nothing but integers comes back.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.41
name: a-crash-inside-code-is-a-fault
kind: decision
is: When the processor faults inside a code leaf or a host function a program called, the walker goes on and the program has the fault crash, caught by recover and seen by outcome like any other. The handler runs on its own stack and returns to the walker's loop, whose state is the region's machine record and not the C stack, so nothing of the walker is lost. A fault outside such code ends the process. Observed on 2026-11-25: a read of address 0, machine code that recurses off its stack and a host function given a bad address were each caught, in any order, a hundred times in a loop.
status: inferred
from: 5.9.3 5.14.6 5.14.40 7.13
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: 5.9.3 asks for a fault handler. Trying machine code and host functions while a system runs is only safe if getting one wrong costs a fault and not the process.
flip: 5.9.3 says the handler records and does not resume; this one resumes the walker, which it can because the walker keeps no state outside the region. What a host function changed before it faulted stays changed.
limit: Saving the return point once per run and a mark around each call of code; the mark costs the per-node code step about a seventh of its speed.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.42
name: counting-is-a-choice-that-counts
kind: decision
is: Entry 99, range, answers the integers from a up to b one at a time: one choice point that holds the next integer instead of a list of options, re-entered by a fail like any choice and closed by a cut. It keeps no activation, so going through a list or a leaf by index needs no recursion. Observed on 2026-11-25: three million integers gathered in 0.09 s with one activation in use; with the lens, the ice and the region programs counting this way the lens's round trip of 70 files takes 0.24 s where it took 0.29 s.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.7 5.14.6 7.13
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Every loop over indices was a recipe calling itself, one activation and one choice point a round, and a loop that does not return keeps them all.
limit: Other loops are still recursions; one whose call stands in the last position runs in the room of one round (5.14.44).
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.43
name: let-gives-a-part-a-place
kind: decision
is: Entry 100, let, evaluates a value and runs its body on the same arguments with one argument, numbered in the node, being that value. The number is a level: it means the same through all of a recipe's body. Self, the cut scope and the bindings are the enclosing recipe's. It replaced a let that was a call of a recipe written in place, under which nesting renumbered the arguments. Observed on 2026-11-25: a program in form rewrote the old lets of 26 stored files into this one, and every ground program ended as before.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.12 5.2.4 5.14.6 7.13
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Bound variables are referred to by position (5.2.4), and a position that moves with nesting cannot carry a name. A let that keeps the arguments it stands among is also what a compiler wants: a value in a slot, with no call.
flip: Each let makes an activation that copies the arguments it stands among. Where that copy is felt, the activation can be extended in place when nothing can return to its older form.
limit: A let takes one value; several are lets inside each other, which the lens writes flat.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.14.44
name: a-loop-runs-in-the-room-of-one-round
kind: decision
is: A call or a let in the last position gives back the activations made since the frame it answers to, unless an open choice or the user of an implementation could return to them. Observed on 2026-11-26: three million rounds of a recursion, by self, by a bound entry and through lets, each ended with 5 activations in use where there had been three million; with a choice left open in every round they are all kept, and failing back into them gave every answer. A recipe called with answers passes on its caller's user, so it can be given back; before, it kept its caller alive as its own.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.6 5.14.43 5.4.9 7.13
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Loops are written as recursions here. Without this every round of one stayed until the loop returned, and a let made it worse.
flip: Nodes a run made are still never given back, so the store is the bound a long loop meets next.
limit: Witnessed by ground/loop-in-place.ck and ground/user-kept.ck, each turned red by a planted fault. One machine.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.14.45
name: a-scope-above-the-program
kind: decision
is: Entry 101, outside, evaluates a node as if asked from outside the run: what it binds goes in a table of its own, above all of the program's bindings. Every layer of the program sees it, implementations bound earlier included; no fail undoes it; it runs on the outside bindings made before it and on the program as its user sees the program. An activation's limit holds both counts. Observed on 2026-11-26: a diagnostic on add saw one use when the program bound it and both, one of them inside an implementation, when bound from outside; it stayed through a fail and was released from outside.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.19 5.14.24 5.4.8 7.21 7.13
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6 2.30.11
conf: mid
why: A program's binding is seen only by what stands above it, so a diagnostic made late misses everything inside what was bound before. Watching and swapping from outside need a scope that is no part of the run's history: not layered into it and not undone with it.
flip: An outside binding is the one thing a fail does not put back, with notes. A search that binds from outside leaves that standing when it fails.
limit: 127 at once. Bindings made together outside do not see each other as a layer's do. Witnessed by ground/from-outside.ck, turned red by three planted faults.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.14.46
name: a-running-program-can-be-asked
kind: decision
is: A second process writes a node's print and its length in the region; between two steps the walker evaluates it as (outcome (outside x)), writes the outcome's print back and goes on where it was. So a request reads any cell and calls anything bound, what it binds is bound from outside the run, and nothing else it did stays. The asker is a program in form that maps the region through the host's own functions. Observed on 2026-11-26: it put a counter on every addition of the long recursion, read 21,787 and then 43,701, took it off, and the recursion answered 2178309.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.45 5.14.40 5.7.6 7.21 7.13
src: 2.30.7 2.30.11 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Diagnostics is reading the region; this is the one step past reading, and it needs nothing in the walker but a look at one count a step, since everything a run is lies in the region between two steps.
flip: The asker waits by looking once a millisecond, where 5.4.11 asks for an event. Anything that can map the region can ask.
limit: One asker at a time. Code that runs long without returning to the walker delays the answer. The first run of this flow answered wrongly and so found an older fault: a lazy bound while an operation's arguments were being evaluated was given their answers (ground/bound-in-flight.ck).
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.14.47
name: asked-to-make-itself-faster
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-26. A recursion that takes 14.3 s on the walker was asked by a second process, one second after it began, to load the emitter and the watcher and to start watching. It ended at 1.3 s with the same answer, 39088169. The request's bindings were made from outside: five of them, the emitter, the watcher's two definitions, the code for the recursion and the watcher's place on call. An implementation bound from outside acts from outside, so the watcher released and renewed its own place there, and bindings made together outside see each other.
status: observed
src: 2.30.7 2.30.11 2.30.6
limit: One machine, one recursion over numbers, which is what the emitter can compile. Calls already begun on the walker finish on the walker; only calls made after the code is bound run it.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.14.48
name: code-and-walker-are-held-equal-by-a-program
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-26. ground/code-or-walker.ck runs twenty-three recipes on the walker and again after asking for their code and holds the outcomes equal: nested lets, large and negative integers, division by zero and of the most negative number, tests with a nothing inside, and three the emitter refuses. The emitter was first written again from one recipe of bare numbers into named definitions and gave byte for byte the same code for seven recipes; then it was extended. Six faults planted in it each turned the program red.
status: observed
src: 2.30.6 2.30.7
limit: The program's first version saw none of the planted faults: its helper was a bound entry, which runs on the bindings beneath its own and so never saw the code. The numbers are 64-bit integers only. One machine.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.14.49
name: bound-says-what-runs-an-entry
kind: decision
is: Entry 102, bound, answers the newest binding of an entry or a recipe as the top of the program sees it, as a node: the entry, its implementation, and whether that runs on its own binding. A program can read what is bound; the emitter takes an entry's recipe from it and compiles a call by the recipe's own name only where that name means the recipe itself, which is so for a binding made in a layer.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.19 5.14.26 7.13
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Without it a program could bind and release but not look, and the emitter had to be handed the recipe again.
flip: It answers the top's view and not the asker's layer. An implementation asking about its own entry gets itself.
limit: Code bound for an entry lies above its recipe as any binding does, so definitions bound earlier, its own layer's among them, still run the recipe on the walker.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.14.50
name: a-call-held-back-until-the-search-has-settled
kind: decision
is: Entry 103, after, takes a recipe and values and makes the call when every choice open at that moment has been closed; a fail that returns past it first, or a fault that leaves the recover it stands in, drops it. Calls held back are words in the region with a mark in each choice point, and are made between two steps as soon as no choice is open, or when the run ends with an answer. Observed on 2026-11-26: of five tries in a search, each holding back a line to a file, the file held the one line of the try that held; a line held before a fault was not written.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.8 5.4.2 5.14.40 5.14.6 7.13
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6 2.30.11
conf: mid
why: 5.4.8 names buffering until the commit as the way an irreversible effect may stand inside a choice. Taking the values at once and making the call later keeps strict order of evaluation (5.4.12) and needs no activation kept.
flip: The rule is absolute, no choice open at all, and not relative to the choices open when it was held back; under an all or an outcome a held call is never made. A held call cannot give its answer to the search.
limit: Witnessed by ground/held-back.ck, run in the open by make check and turned red by two planted faults. Question 6.1.15 stays the guide's: this builds one of its three answers beside the refusal.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.14.51
name: reading-and-printing-in-form
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-26. form/read.ck and form/print.ck are entries 76 and 77 written in form from the page of the encoding: the reader checks each leaf's bytes itself and names the first rule broken, and both keep their numbering in notes. Bound in place of the bootstrap's own, they hold the 142 vectors, the lens shows and reads all 83 files of nodes through them, and every ground program ends as before. A fault planted in each was reported by the vectors. Entry 104, nothing, lets the reader refuse as the bootstrap's does: an answer of nothing with the rule's word as its reason.
status: observed
src: 2.30.3 2.30.6 2.30.7
limit: One hand wrote the page, the bootstrap's reader and this one, so it is no second reader in the sense of 7.2. Nodes are still made by the bootstrap's pack and join, the id is the bootstrap's SHA-256, and the bytes' reader, decode, is not written in form. Running the ground programs on it found an older fault of the walker: step evaluated its node in the activation of whatever computed it (ground/step-stands-here.ck).
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.14.52
name: a-recipe-closed-over-values
kind: decision
is: Entry 105, close, answers a recipe closed over values: a data node of entry 106 that call, recover and after take wherever they take a recipe. Called, it runs the recipe with its values first among the arguments; self inside it is the closed node; closing a closed node adds values after the inner ones. The lens writes an inner recipe that uses an outer name as the recipe closed over the outer arguments (5.15.7). Observed on 2026-11-26: closed over three and over nine values, calling itself, handed to another definition and to recover.
status: inferred
from: 5.1.1 5.2.4 5.14.43 5.3.5 7.13
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: A recipe is a node and holds no values (5.1.1), so what an inner recipe needs from outside has to be given to it; a node that pairs the recipe with those values keeps both immutable and needs no new kind of activation.
flip: Each closing makes a node that is never given back, so a closing inside a long loop fills the store as a counter does. What is bound for the recipe runs a closed one too, with more arguments than its code was written for, which code then declines.
limit: Witnessed by ground/closes-over.ck, turned red by two planted faults. At most forty arguments in all.
at: 2026-11-26
